<Layout title="UK Stamp Duty (SDLT) Explained: A Buyer's Guide" description="Everything first-time buyers and home movers need to know about UK Stamp Duty Land Tax.">
  <main class="container">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>UK Stamp Duty Explained: A First-Time Buyer's Guide</h1>
      <p class="meta">Written by the Engineering Team at Finance Hub</p>

      <p>If you are buying a property in England or Northern Ireland, you will likely have to pay Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT). It is a significant upfront cost that catches many first-time buyers off guard.</p>

      <h2>The First-Time Buyer Relief</h2>
      <p>The UK government offers generous relief to first-time buyers to help them get on the property ladder. Under current rules, you pay <strong>0% Stamp Duty</strong> on the first £425,000 of the property price. You then pay 5% on the portion between £425,001 and £625,000.</p>
      <p>You can quickly calculate your exact bill using our <a href="/tools/uk-stamp-duty-calculator">UK Stamp Duty Calculator</a>.</p>

      <h2>When Does the Relief End?</h2>
      <p>If the property costs more than £625,000, you lose the first-time buyer relief entirely and must pay standard rates. This is a critical threshold for buyers in London and the South East.</p>

      <h2>Standard Rates for Home Movers</h2>
      <p>If you are not a first-time buyer, the rules are different. You pay 0% up to £250,000, 5% between £250,001 and £925,000, and 10% above that. Always use a calculator before putting in an offer so you know exactly how much cash you need to complete the purchase.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
